e better, but anyway, I love the, I love pistachio, I love chocolate.\nTurn 50. STUDENT: Okay.\nTurn 51. TUTOR: Anyway, uh, yeah, uh, Mia, for you, I will try some Dubai chocolate in Chicago and I'll take some pictures and I'll let you know if it's really tasty. Like if it's actually better. So, I'll let you know.\nTurn 52. TUTOR: All right, Mia. Um, you know what, let's go ahead and play some math games. Do you would you like to do, um, let's see, an educational platform or...\nTurn 53. STUDENT: What is that calculator thing or something?\nTurn 54. TUTOR: You want to do the calculator game? Yes. I can totally do that. Let me see if I can find it.\nTurn 55. TUTOR: Okay. Uh, let me have a look. Is it the one where like, it's missing, it's the broken calculator where it's missing like...\nTurn 56. STUDENT: Yeah.\nTurn 57. TUTOR: That one? Okay. Let me share my screen. We can totally do that.\nTurn 58. TUTOR: [SCREEN_INTERACTION: tutor - sharing screen] Tutor shares her screen. The screen share area is black.\nTurn 58. TUTOR: All right, so I think it's this one you're talking about.\nTurn 59. STUDENT: Yeah.\nTurn 60. TUTOR: You you have to find ways to make a number, right? Okay.\nTurn 61. STUDENT: But in a kid way.\nTurn 62. TUTOR: All right. So, can you think of a way to make forty-four?\nTurn 63. TUTOR: [PROBLEM_CHANGE: p1 - Broken Calculator (start)] The screen shows a math game called \"Broken Calculator\". The target number is 44. A calculator is shown with numbers 0, 1, 2, 3, 5, 6, 7, 8, 9, and operators +, -, x, ÷. The number 4 is missing.\nTutor types \"33+\" on the calculator.\nTutor types \"10\" on the calculator.\nTutor presses the equals button.\nTutor types \"+1\" on the calculator.\nTutor presses the equals button. The answer is 44. The game shows \"Perfect!\".\nTurn 63. STUDENT: Is there a zero? Yes, there is. Okay.\nTurn 64. STUDENT: Thirty-three plus ten.\nTurn 65. TUTOR: Yeah.\nTurn 66. STUDENT: Yeah. Ten.\nTurn 67. TUTOR: Oh, yeah.\nTurn 68. STUDENT